  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nAn upper shortwave trough is currently sliding across the Central \nPlains with an additional shortwave over the Great Lakes. Weak upper \nridging is over the southeastern US as well as the southwestern US. \nShowers and storms have dissipated over our area with activity still \nongoing farther south in Oklahoma as weak 700 mb WAA continues. \nCooler temperatures are being observed given the residual cloud \ncover over much of eastern KS and the rainfall from this morning. \nHigh temperatures range from the 70s in southeast KS to the mid 80s \nin central KS. As we move into tonight, cloud cover will continue to \ndecrease with light easterly/southeasterly winds. Given the moisture \nfrom rainfall, patchy fog looks likely to develop late tonight \nthrough Friday morning especially for areas along and east of the \nKansas Turnpike.  \n\nA warm-up will start again on Friday as high temperatures are \nforecast to return to the upper 80s to the lower 90s. Mostly dry \nconditions are expected during the day Friday before chances for \nshowers and storms will start to increase again towards late Friday \nnight mainly towards the overnight hours. A LLJ is progged to \nincrease across western KS with the nose of the jet towards northern \nKS. A cold front will start to push south through Nebraska late \nFriday night with sufficient instability, potentially allowing \nstorms to develop along it as it makes its way to southern Nebraska. \nThese storms are anticipated to move south into Kansas, bringing \nanother potential round of rain to our area. However, there is \nuncertainty as the ECMWF, GFS, and HRRR are the only solutions that \nsupport this whereas other guidance keeps Nebraska and Kansas dry \nFriday night through Saturday morning. If storms do develop, severe \nweather appears possible given around 40 kts of effective shear is \nprogged. Trends with this activity will continue to be monitored. \n\nFor Saturday, the cold front is expected to stall out across \nnorthern KS into portions of central KS. Just north of the boundary, \nhighs are forecast to be in the low to mid 80s with temperatures \nsouth of the boundary in the lower 90s. By late afternoon, diurnal \nheating will lead to plenty of instability developing, making \nanother chance for storms possible. Given the steep low-level lapse \nrates, and DCAPE values over 1300 J/kg, damaging wind gusts would be \nthe primary concern with this activity, though large hail cannot be \nruled out given steep mid-level lapse rates and 40 kts of effective \nshear. Chances for storms may linger into Saturday night and Sunday \nmorning as the nocturnal LLJ ramps up. By Sunday afternoon, lee \nsurface troughing will increase resulting in breezy southerly winds \nacross the area. This will aid in high temperatures returning to the \nlow to mid 90s on Sunday afternoon. Mostly dry conditions are \nforecast for Sunday night into Monday morning as rain chances are \nprogged to stay north of the area.\n\nFor Monday and beyond, an upper trough over the northwestern US will \npropagate northeast towards the Northern Plains by Monday afternoon. \nAs this trough continues to slide across the Northern Plains, it \nwill push a cold front south into Nebraska by Monday afternoon. Lee \ntroughing will remain in place across Kansas with breezy southerly \nwinds anticipated. Another hot day is in store for Monday as highs \nonce again rise into the mid 90s. This cold front will continue to \npush south, likely allowing showers and storms to develop along it, \nincreasing chances for Monday night into Tuesday. By late Tuesday \nafternoon, the front will likely make it to southern Kansas. This \nwill result in highs ranging from the 70s in northern Kansas to the \n90s in far southern Kansas. Following this front, highs are forecast \nto return to the 70s and 80s for Wednesday and Thursday. Periodic \nrain chances are possible for beyond Tuesday as guidance suggests \nadditional shortwaves to slide across the region.",
